window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-29"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "All three of these are the same decomposition: choose the one thing at the top, split what is left into two sides, and combine them. I want to say that out loud first, because it means the third part is not a new problem and I should not design it as one.", "questions": ["Do two shapes that mirror each other count as distinct, or as one shape?", "Should repeated values from different groupings be reported once or once per grouping?", "How large an input do I need to support, given that both the counts and the number of groupings grow very fast?"], "assumptions": ["Shapes are distinct when they branch in different places, so a shape and its mirror image are two shapes.", "Values are reported once each, sorted ascending, because what the caller wants to know is whether the set has more than one element.", "Sizes beyond a documented maximum are a typed failure rather than an attempt, and the counting maximum and the listing maximum are different numbers for different reasons.", "An expression must alternate value and operator and begin and end with a value; anything else is malformed rather than something to interpret."], "edgeCases": ["a tree of no nodes at all, which is the base case both of the first two parts stand on", "a size one past the documented maximum, which must fail rather than overflow", "an expression that is a single value, which has nothing to group", "an expression whose groupings all agree, which must report one value and not several copies", "a token list that starts or ends with an operator, or has two in a row"], "restate": "Given a rule tree size, report how many distinct shapes it can take and list them; and given a policy expression whose operator grouping is unspecified, report every value it could evaluate to."}, "solution": {"pitch": "One decomposition, three objectives. Counting fills an array indexed by size, where each entry sums the products over every way of dividing the remaining nodes. Listing does the same division but builds structures. Evaluating splits a token range at an operator instead of splitting a size, which is the same idea with a richer memo key, and that step from a size to a range is the interesting part of the problem.", "dataStructures": ["One array indexed by size for the counting, filled ascending so both sides of every division are already there.", "One array of shape lists per size for the listing, which is the same fill carrying structures instead of numbers.", "A dictionary keyed on a token range for the evaluation, which is what keeps the same sub-expression from being solved once per grouping that reaches it.", "A set for the values produced within a range, so two groupings landing on the same number collapse where they are produced rather than at the end."], "types": ["A shape is an indirect enum with an empty case and a branching case, which derives equality and hashing for free and makes the tests exact rather than approximate.", "A token is an enum with an associated value on the value case, so a malformed expression is a shape the type system lets me detect rather than a parse to reverse engineer.", "The error enum separates a negative size, an out-of-range size, an empty expression and a malformed one, because those are four different things for a caller to do something about."], "composition": "The counting part and the listing part are implemented independently, and the suite asserts they agree at every size it can list. That equality is worth more than one calling the other, because it can be red where a call would be green: it proves the two parts agree about what a shape is. The evaluation part reuses the decomposition rather than the code, which is the honest description of the relationship.", "tradeoff": "There is a closed form for these counts that runs in linear time, and a binomial form that is constant time. I am not using either, because neither adapts to the constrained variations this decomposition exists to support, and the quadratic convolution is the version that generalises. Worth naming out loud rather than leaving the interviewer to wonder whether I know the closed form.", "trap": "Overflow. Both the counts and the number of groupings explode, and an integer overflow in Swift ends the process rather than returning a wrong number, so the guard has to fire before the arithmetic rather than after it. The second trap is reusing the counting ceiling for the listing: what limits listing is memory, and it is a far smaller number."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the counting fill first, with the base case and the range guard before any arithmetic runs.", "Print the first few counts and check them against the sequence before moving on, because a wrong base case is invisible otherwise.", "Write the listing as the same ascending fill carrying structures, and state the order out loud as you write the loops.", "Write the validation for the expression next, since the recursion is much simpler when it can trust its input.", "Write the memoised split over token ranges, then the combination step with its checked arithmetic.", "Finish by sorting the collected values, and say why the deduplication is the point rather than a tidy-up."], "commentary": ["The empty structure counts as one, not zero. That is the single most consequential line here and I want it visible before anything else.", "One node is spent at the top, which is why the two sides account for one fewer than the size I am filling.", "I check the size before the multiply rather than after. An overflow here ends the process, so a check afterwards is a check that never runs.", "I am fixing the order now: ascending left side, and within that the order the sides were already produced. Leaving it implicit would make this useless as a fixture generator.", "This part gets its own ceiling. What stops me listing is memory, not what fits in an integer, and those are very different numbers.", "This is the same split as before. What changed is that the thing at the split point is an operator rather than a node, so the memo key goes from a size to a range.", "I split only at operator positions, because a range beginning with an operator is not an expression and there is nothing for the recursion to do with it.", "I return the values sorted and deduplicated, because the question the caller is really asking is whether more than one value is possible."], "checkIn": "Before the third part, say: this is the same decomposition as the first two, with an operator at the split point instead of a node. Do you want me to write it that way, or would you rather I treated it as a fresh problem and derived it from scratch?"}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk the counts up from the base: no nodes is one, one node is one, two nodes is two, three nodes is five, and four nodes is fourteen, which is the sequence to check against before trusting anything else.", "Walk the listing at two nodes: dividing zero on the left gives one shape whose right side branches again, and dividing one on the left gives its mirror, so exactly two shapes come out in that order.", "Walk the worked expression, two times three minus four times five: three operators means five groupings, and they evaluate to minus thirty-four, minus fourteen, minus ten, minus ten and ten, so four distinct values come back sorted.", "Walk one range of the memo by hand: the middle three tokens are solved once and read back by both of the outer splits that need them, which is the whole reason a nine-operator expression is instant."], "tests": "Run swift test --filter Problem29RuleGroupingAnalyzerTests and read the three suites in part order. The size-zero test in the first suite is what catches the wrong base case, the test comparing the listing against the count at every size is what catches the two parts disagreeing, and the nine-operator test in the third suite is what catches a missing memo.", "complexity": "The counting fill is quadratic in the size with linear space. The listing does the same work but its output is the count itself, which grows exponentially, so its cost is what it has to emit rather than what it has to visit. The evaluation solves a quadratic number of token ranges once each, and the work in a range is the product of the two sides' distinct value counts, which is why deduplicating inside the recursion rather than at the end matters.", "moreTime": "Restricting which sides may pair up turns the same convolution into a constrained count and is where the closed form stops being usable, which is the honest argument for having written the quadratic version. Reporting which grouping produced each value needs the recursion to carry the grouping alongside the value, which is the listing part and the evaluation part fused. Counting groupings that reach each value rather than listing values is the same recursion with a sum where the set was."}, "code": "/// The shape of a rule tree, with the rule data stripped away. Two trees have\n/// the same shape when they branch in the same places.\n///\n/// Deriving Hashable is the reason this is an indirect enum rather than a class:\n/// tests can compare shapes and put them in a set with no equality code at all.\npublic indirect enum RuleShape: Hashable, Sendable {\n    case empty\n    case node(left: RuleShape, right: RuleShape)\n}\n\n/// One token of a policy expression. A well-formed expression alternates a\n/// value, an operator, a value, and so on, beginning and ending with a value.\npublic enum ExpressionToken: Equatable, Sendable {\n    case value(Int)\n    case add\n    case subtract\n    case multiply\n}\n\npublic enum GroupingError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case negativeNodeCount\n    case countOverflow\n    case emptyExpression\n    case malformedExpression\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct RuleGroupingAnalyzer: Sendable {\n    /// The largest node count whose shape count still fits in an Int. Counting\n    /// past this would overflow, and an Int overflow in Swift is a trap that\n    /// takes the process down rather than a wrong answer, so the guard has to\n    /// fire before the multiply rather than after it.\n    public static let maximumCountableNodeCount = 33\n\n    /// The largest node count worth enumerating. The shapes are counted by the\n    /// same numbers Part 1 produces, so this ceiling is about what fits in\n    /// memory, not about what fits in an Int.\n    public static let maximumEnumerableNodeCount = 12\n\n    public init() {}\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Count distinct rule-tree shapes\n\n    /// How many distinct shapes a rule tree with `nodeCount` internal nodes can\n    /// take.\n    ///\n    /// One node is distinguished - the root - and it is consumed by the split,\n    /// which is where the minus one comes from: the remaining nodes divide\n    /// between the two sides in every possible way, and each way contributes the\n    /// product of the two sides' counts. A tree of no nodes has exactly one\n    /// shape, the empty one, and reading that base case as zero collapses every\n    /// later entry to nothing.\n    public func shapeCount(nodeCount: Int) throws(GroupingError) -> Int {\n        guard nodeCount >= 0 else { throw .negativeNodeCount }\n        guard nodeCount <= Self.maximumCountableNodeCount else { throw .countOverflow }\n\n        var counts = Array(repeating: 0, count: nodeCount + 1)\n        counts[0] = 1\n        guard nodeCount >= 1 else { return counts[nodeCount] }\n        for size in 1...nodeCount {\n            counts[size] = (0..<size).reduce(0) { running, leftSize in\n                running + counts[leftSize] * counts[size - 1 - leftSize]\n            }\n        }\n        return counts[nodeCount]\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Enumerate the shapes\n\n    /// Every distinct shape a rule tree with `nodeCount` internal nodes can take.\n    ///\n    /// The same split as Part 1, building structures where Part 1 built numbers.\n    /// The order is fixed on purpose so callers and tests can rely on it:\n    /// ascending left size, and within a left size the two sides in the order\n    /// this method already produced them.\n    public func shapes(nodeCount: Int) throws(GroupingError) -> [RuleShape] {\n        guard nodeCount >= 0 else { throw .negativeNodeCount }\n        guard nodeCount <= Self.maximumEnumerableNodeCount else { throw .countOverflow }\n\n        // Filling ascending means both sides of every split are already built by\n        // the time the split needs them, so nothing here has to recurse.\n        var built: [[RuleShape]] = [[.empty]]\n        guard nodeCount >= 1 else { return built[0] }\n        for size in 1...nodeCount {\n            var atSize: [RuleShape] = []\n            for leftSize in 0..<size {\n                for left in built[leftSize] {\n                    for right in built[size - 1 - leftSize] {\n                        atSize.append(.node(left: left, right: right))\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n            built.append(atSize)\n        }\n        return built[nodeCount]\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - Every result an ungrouped expression can produce\n\n    /// Every value the expression can evaluate to, over every way of grouping it.\n    ///\n    /// Part 2 split a size into a left size and a right size. This splits a token\n    /// range at an operator. It is the same split; only what sits at the split\n    /// point changed, and the memo key changes with it from one number to a pair\n    /// of indices.\n    ///\n    /// The results come back sorted with duplicates removed, because two\n    /// different groupings landing on the same value is exactly the case the\n    /// caller wants collapsed: what matters is whether the set has more than one\n    /// element.\n    public func possibleResults(_ tokens: [ExpressionToken]) throws(GroupingError) -> [Int] {\n        guard !tokens.isEmpty else { throw .emptyExpression }\n        try validateAlternation(tokens)\n\n        var memo: [Range<Int>: [Int]] = [:]\n        let results = try resultSet(tokens, 0..<tokens.count, &memo)\n        return results.sorted()\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared machinery\n\n    /// A well-formed expression has a value at every even position, an operator\n    /// at every odd one, and therefore an odd length.\n    private func validateAlternation(_ tokens: [ExpressionToken]) throws(GroupingError) {\n        guard tokens.count % 2 == 1 else { throw .malformedExpression }\n        for (position, token) in tokens.enumerated() {\n            let isValue: Bool\n            switch token {\n            case .value: isValue = true\n            case .add, .subtract, .multiply: isValue = false\n            }\n            guard isValue == (position % 2 == 0) else { throw .malformedExpression }\n        }\n    }\n\n    /// Every distinct value the tokens in `range` can produce, memoised on the\n    /// range so each sub-expression is solved once however many groupings reach\n    /// it.\n    private func resultSet(\n        _ tokens: [ExpressionToken],\n        _ range: Range<Int>,\n        _ memo: inout [Range<Int>: [Int]]\n    ) throws(GroupingError) -> Set<Int> {\n        if let cached = memo[range] { return Set(cached) }\n\n        // A range of one token is a bare value: there is nothing left to group.\n        if range.count == 1, case .value(let number) = tokens[range.lowerBound] {\n            memo[range] = [number]\n            return [number]\n        }\n\n        var results: Set<Int> = []\n        for split in stride(from: range.lowerBound + 1, to: range.upperBound, by: 2) {\n            let left = try resultSet(tokens, range.lowerBound..<split, &memo)\n            let right = try resultSet(tokens, (split + 1)..<range.upperBound, &memo)\n            for leftValue in left {\n                for rightValue in right {\n                    results.insert(try combine(leftValue, tokens[split], rightValue))\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        memo[range] = Array(results)\n        return results\n    }\n\n    /// One operator applied to one pair of values.\n    ///\n    /// Every arithmetic step is checked rather than written plainly. An Int\n    /// overflow traps in Swift, and a grouping that overflows is a caller's\n    /// expression the service cannot evaluate, which is a typed failure and not\n    /// a reason to bring the process down.\n    private func combine(_ left: Int, _ token: ExpressionToken, _ right: Int) throws(GroupingError) -> Int {\n        let outcome: (partialValue: Int, overflow: Bool)\n        switch token {\n        case .add: outcome = left.addingReportingOverflow(right)\n        case .subtract: outcome = left.subtractingReportingOverflow(right)\n        case .multiply: outcome = left.multipliedReportingOverflow(by: right)\n        case .value: throw .malformedExpression\n        }\n        guard !outcome.overflow else { throw .countOverflow }\n        return outcome.partialValue\n    }\n}\n"};
